><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250522133217+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250522133221+10'00'" name="72b49601-c315-469b-8ad9-6c9a22acde53" page="3" coords="64.999985,525.897095,306.566589,525.897095,64.999985,515.960815,306.566589,515.960815,56.499985,515.896851,306.602295,515.896851,56.499985,505.960571,306.602295,505.960571,56.499985,505.896606,306.588226,505.896606,56.499985,495.960327,306.588226,495.960327,56.499985,495.896362,306.570801,495.896362,56.499985,485.960083,306.570801,485.960083,56.499985,485.896118,306.561432,485.896118,56.499985,475.959839,306.561432,475.959839,56.499985,475.895874,306.558167,475.895874,56.499985,465.959595,306.558167,465.959595,56.499985,465.895630,306.576630,465.895630,56.499985,455.959351,306.576630,455.959351,56.499985,455.895386,306.569977,455.895386,56.499985,445.959106,306.569977,445.959106,56.499985,445.895142,306.574341,445.895142,56.499985,435.958862,306.574341,435.958862,56.499985,435.894897,306.573303,435.894897,56.499985,425.958618,306.573303,425.958618,56.499985,425.894653,306.586914,425.894653,56.499985,415.958374,306.586914,415.958374,56.499985,415.894409,306.564758,415.894409,56.499985,405.958130,306.564758,405.958130,56.499985,405.894165,202.041458,405.894165,56.499985,395.957886,202.041458,395.957886" rect="56.189468,395.647369,306.912811,526.207642" subject="Comment on Text" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-11.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>In conclusion, our data clearly demonstrates the high variability&#xD;between athletes following an intravenous iron infusion, and&#xD;therefore, we recommend an individualised approach to follow-up&#xD;testing. We suggest that a 1- and 6-month follow-up blood screening&#xD;should occur to assess the efficacy of an intravenous iron&#xD;infusion and the individual rate of decay. Of note, it should be&#xD;emphasised that this investigation was an exploratory exercise&#xD;based on limited data, and therefore, there remains a need for&#xD;prospective studies to confirm our findings. Future research&#xD;should employ a consistent 2-year follow-up period to capture the&#xD;full extent of the decay in serum ferritin, and should account for&#xD;factors such as haemoglobin levels, training load, dietary iron&#xD;intake, and oral iron supplement use.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="3" rect="621.000000,433.897095,805.000000,525.897095"
/></highlight
